<title>FRANCE: French crude oil imports fell in February.</title>
<headline>French crude oil imports fell in February.</headline>
<dateline>PARIS 1997-04-18</dateline>
<text>
<p>France's industry ministry issued the following data for deliveries to refineries, refining margins and capacity utilisation.</p>
<p>Crude deliveries are by origin ('000 tonnes).</p>
<p>					 Feb 1997   Jan 1997   Feb 1996</p>
<p>    Total				 7,111	7,619	7,134</p>
<p>    Middle East			 2,845	2,269	2,301</p>
<p>    including Saudi Arabia	  1,424	1,654	1,749</p>
<p>		  Iraq			571	  271	    0</p>
<p>		  Iran			497	  258	  512</p>
<p>		  Kuwait		    272	   10	    0</p>
<p>    Africa				  943	1,136	1,125</p>
<p>    including Algeria		   522	  444	   79</p>
<p>		  Libya			 0	    0	  160</p>
<p>		  Nigeria		   316	  397	  676</p>
<p>    OECD Europe			 2,523	3,150	3,079</p>
<p>    including Britain		   945	1,281	1,334</p>
<p>		  Norway		  1,439	1,731	1,532</p>
<p>		  France		    139	  138	  160</p>
<p>    Other				   799	1,064	  627</p>
<p>    including Venezuela		   0	    0	    0</p>
<p>		  Mexico			0	    0	    0</p>
<p>		  ex-USSR		   799	1,064	  627</p>
<p>The ministry also reiterated the following data for French refining margins, based on North Sea Brent crude ($/barrel).</p>
<p>			    Feb 1997  Jan 1997  Dec 1996  Feb 1996</p>
<p>    Refining Margin     1.59	2.15	2.19	2.64</p>
<p>In the most recent month for which refinery capacity utilisation is available, French plants ran at 96 percent of capacity in January, up from 89 percent in the strike-hit month of December but down from 97 percent in January 1996.</p>
<p>-- Paris newsroom +331 4221 5146</p>
</text>
